'''Araucaria''' ({{Lang-ku|Pucará}}; meaning "fortified place"; [[Maroniko]]: ''Arakawia'') is a {{W|confederation|confederated}} [[Communities of Balisca|community]] within [[Balisca]]. The community takes its name from the [[w:Araucaria|eponymous trees]] that are abundant across it's territory. With an area of {{convert|168372.70|km2|0|abbr=on}}, it is the 13th largest territory in [[W:Latin America|Latin America]] and the 90th largest in the world. To the north it is bordered by [[Hesperia]], surrounds the [[Castejón Metropolis]] on three sides, and is bounded by the [[Aethiopian Ocean]] to the west and [[Asikyira]] to the south. It is bisected by the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]]. With over twenty-three million inhabitants in 2023, it is the third most populous of the Baliscan confederacy. Araucaria is home to Balisca’s fifth largest metropolitan area, [[Axarquía]], which also serves as the ''de facto'' Araucarian capital. Other large cities in the region include [[Yerba Buena]], [[León]], [[Carabassa]], [[Porciúncula]], and [[Jatiel]]. It has eight regions; [[Arapytúcaá]], [[Juyú]], [[Brasal]], [[Hibiscara]], [[Cabu Traïdor]], [[Jaguarundi]], [[Tukupi]] and [[Luzamontaña]].

Araucaria is home to one of the largest populations of people of Kaftian descent in the [[Algarves]], with a significant minority of its inhabitants being descendants of Kaftian slaves brought to Balisca in the colonial era. Additionally, it is home to significant minorities Indigenous people (including those who inhabited the land prior to the Illypnian colonization and those who were displaced afterwards) mostly the [[Kurupiranga]], and [[Mozbayán|Mozbayáns]].

The area that today corresponds to the confederacy's territory was already inhabited by various [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|indigenous peoples]] from approximately 12,000 BC. In the early 16th century, the coast of the region was visited by [[Xacan|Xacano]] explorers and navigators. Illypnian colonization of the land was quite limited as a result of it's rugged terrain, and vicious attacks by indigenous peoples. The land remained largely untouched by non-Indigenous peoples until the establishment of [[Daramazán]] 1617 by escaped [[Moro people|Moro]] convicts and self-liberated Kaftian slaves. Due to its relatively brutal reputation, it was prohibitively known to colonists as [[Maroon Confederacy|'''Satanazes''']] ("isle of devils"), and a re quickly became a haven for [[W:Maroons|runaways]], [[Maroon pirates|pirates]], and those [[Maroon Wars|actively fighting to end slavery]]. The [[Baliscan Civil War]] and the [[Kafto-Ethiopian Charter]] (which {{W|de jure}} recognized Araucaria as a state of former slaves and their descendants) brought significant demographic changes to the region. Araucaria was the last Baliscan polity to initiate a process of {{W|industrialization}}, but it rapidly caught up with the rest of Balisca following it's [[1883 Confederal Convention|confederation in 1883]], and its population became one of the most [[w:Urbanization|urbanized]] within the Confederation.

Araucaria is an {{W|advanced economy|advanced}} {{W|Participatory economics|participatory economy}} with a long history of {{W|economic democracy}}; if considered a sovereign state, it would be the ?th largest economy in the world. Major sectors of the Araucarian economy include transportation, education, information technology and research, government services, construction, and tourism. 

Araucaria has maintained one of the fastest growing populations within Balisca since 2000, with the [[2025 Baliscan census]] confirming the fastest population growth in the nation since 2010. [[Axarquía]] was the fastest-growing metropolitan area in Balisca from 2000 to 2009. The community is the largest producer of apples, hops, pears, blueberries, spearmint oil, and sweet cherries in Balisca. It additionally ranks high in the production of apricots, asparagus, dry edible peas, grapes, lentils, peppermint oil, and potatoes.
==History==
{{Main|History of Araucaria}}
===Indigenous history===
====European arrival and Maroon conflict====
{{Main|Maroon Wars|Maroon Confederacy|Volisanya Boba}}

[[File:Palmares massacre.png|left|thumb|The [[Palmares revolt|Palmares uprising]] and subsequent massacre of former slaveowners marked the beginning of the [[Maroon Wars]]]]
In the early 16th century, the coast of the region was visited by Volisanian explorers and navigators. Illypnian colonization of the land was quite limited as a result of it's rugged terrain and attacks by indigenous peoples. The land remained largely unexplored until the establishment of [[Daramazán]] 1617 by escaped Moro convicts and self-liberated Kaftian slaves. Due to its relatively brutal reputation, it was prohibitively known to colonists as [[Maroon Confederacy|'''Satanazes''']] ("isle of devils"), and it became a haven for [[Araucarian Passage|runaways,]] [[Maroon pirates|pirates]], and those [[Maroon Wars|actively fighting to interrupt slavery]]. Originally confined to the [[Western Plains (Balisca)|Bayaran frontier]], this violent upheaval resulted in a substantial migration of people south into ''Pucará'', where it [[Maroon Wars|expanded exponentially]] and adopted [[W:Guerrilla warfare|guerrilla warfare]] to resist colonialist incursions.  

This diverse resistance gradually developed a society that adopted a range of {{W|Indigenous peoples in Balisca|Indigenous}} and [[Southwestern Kaftia|Southwestern Kaftian]] socio-political models, with aspects of [[W:Sephardic Jews|Sephardic]] and [[Mozbayán]] culture and specifically clan adaptations also included. It was decentralized in nature, operating as a {{W|confederation}} of nominally independent towns and settlements united by their resistance to Mesogean colonial encroachment.

[[Daramazán]] was founded as a colony 1617 by escaped Moro convicts and Kaftian slaves, at a time when the Araucaria Territory remained largely unexplored by Illypnians. As a refuge for those fleeing the colonial authorities, the territory became notorious for its gaudy displays of wealth and loose morals. It was a popular homeport for Mozarabic pirates from which to launch their raids on Spanish slave ships and settlements to liberate enslaved people, most of whom then joined the colony.  
[[File:Jaspar_Beckx_(tidl._tilskrevet)_-_Don_Miguel_de_Castro,_Emissary_of_Kongo_-_KMS7_-_Statens_Museum_for_Kunst.jpg|left|thumb|244x244px|[[Nsuukidem Jesumbo]], the Sanyanian ambassador to the [[Maroon Confederacy]] (1642)]]
[[File:Oath of the Ancestors painting.png|thumb|360x360px|[[Oath of the Ancestors|Juramento de los Ancestros]]  (1886) depicts a [[Maroon Wars|Maroon]] (left) and a [[W:Yoruba people|Yoruba]] woman (right) symbolizing the shared [[Afro Atlantic Charter|oath to resist against slavery]]]]
Oba [[Ekpedeme]] of [[Sanyan]] died in 1652, and was replaced by [[Nsuukidem of Sanyan|Nsuukidem]]. Nsuukidem, the son of former nobles, had himself served as an ambassador to the [[Maroon Confederacy]] and witnessed the cruelty of the {{W|chattel slavery}} that Sanyan was facilitating. He strongly denounced the practice, and threatened to end the arrangement. He was convinced otherwise by his advisors, who reminded him of the significant power imbalance between the foreign powers and Sanyan. In order the overcome this weakness, disrupt the slave trade, and quickly allow the prosecution of intercepted slavers and their vessels, Nsuukidem, supported by his council, and through his ties to figures within the [[Araucarian Passage]], financed the [[Maroon pirates]] to halt intercept and liberate Illypnian slave ships on their way to the [[Algarves]]. This was already a common practice of the Maroon pirates dating back to the founding of Daramazán, however as shown by historical records, pirate attacks intensified in the years after 1643.

This mutual alliance between the two sides was solidified by the [[Kafto-Ethiopian Charter]] of 1793 during the conflict, and the two came together to support the [[Asikyiran Revolution]].

The Ìlàlóye trade was significantly reduced with the [[Volisanian Civil War]], and even further with the beginning of the [[Baliscan War of Independence]]. The trade completely evaporated in 1808 with [[Kaftian Squadron]] enforcing the [[Kaftian anti-slave trade campaign]] from [[Joint Base Ajowa|Ajowa Naval Base]] in [[Sanyan]]. Western Kaftian slave markets, through which a majority of slaves sent to the Algarves had passed, largely collapsed following Volisania and Breisland halting the trade in 1815, and the outbreak of the [[Azawagh War]] was the coup de grâce for the slave trade. Oloye [[Kuyikabasi of Sanyan|Kuyikabasi]] officially abolished all forms of slavery that same year.

Following the victory of the Baliscan revolutionaries, the Maroon Confederacy supported Sanyan's efforts to return newly freed Kaftians to their places of origin through the island. The arrangement was a substantial failure on several fronts: the displaced Kaftians who sailed to the island were often unable to ever return to their original homes on the continent. Additionally, upon arrival in Sanyan, each recaptive was given a registration number, and information on their physical qualities would be entered into the {{W|Register of Liberated Africans|Register of Liberated Kaftians}}. Oftentimes the documentation would be overwhelmingly subjective and would result in inaccurate entries, making them difficult to track. Further investigation revealed additional disparities in the entries of the re-captives, specifically in the names; many recaptives felt pressured to adopt [[Imo language|Imo]] or [[Kinza]] names to avoid potential maltreatment, which contributed greatly to the difficulty in locating them after they arrived. 

Between the outbreak of the first slave revolts in 1593, largely in [[Bayara]], and the [[Restaurasionistas|Volisan ''Restaurasionistas'']] attempt at reconquering the territory (the [[Baliscan War of Independence]]) in 1808, more than 375,000 formerly enslaved and free-born black people who faced social and legal oppression in the [[Aethiopic world]], had been liberated. Six Volisan naval expeditions invaded the territory in an attempt to conquer it between 1699 and 1736, but these were all beaten back with considerable loss of life as a result of violent guerilla tactics and a lack of Volisan familiarity with the land. The Confederacy later intervened in the [[Baliscan Civil War]], playing a decisive role in beating back the [[Siege of Castejón]], and in subsequent years developed an amiable relationship with [[Anyos Federales|Reconstructionist Balisca]] with the [[Kafto-Ethiopian Charter]]. The Confederacy continued to exercise sovereignty until it confederated with the [[Balisca|Baliscan Confederation]] at the [[1883 Confederal Convention]].

In [[Volisanya Boba|the period]] following the [[Volisanian Civil War|dissolution]] of the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania]] and up until the opening days of the [[Baliscan War of Independence]], the Confederacy received limited diplomatic recognition from the [[Empire of Burawa]] and maintained extensive trade relations with the [[Hesperian League]]. It is through the merchant of the League that Maroons first learned of the civil war in Volisania.

=== Early industrialization ===
Several men who aspired to become the new industrial magnates of this country would be men such as [[Tomas Casselle]] (who found the [[Araucarian Mining Company|Compañía Minera Araucariana]]), [[Aleix Puigmitjà]] (who pioneered industrialization of Araucaria's hemp production and refining by way of [[Hierbería|'''Hierbería Botica''']]), and [[Pasqual Campas]], who would found the [[Araucarian Mining Company|Compañía]] [[Trans-Araucarian Railway Company|de Trenes Trans-Araucarianos]]. Amongst others, these individuals would play a crucial role in the industrialization of Araucaria, as well as its ability to enter into the status of an {{W|industrialized economy}} by the turn of the century. Araucaria's industrial growth was not without outside help, as received significant assistance from the Baliscan government with the development of the tools and technologies that would make the territory more suitable to the needs of an industrialized economy. 
[[File:Javanese immigrants Araucaria.png.png|thumb|300x300px|[[Kartasugih|Sugihan]] railroad workers at a post office in [[Jatiel]], during the construction of the [[Trans-Atlántido Railroad]]|left]]Making use of its great wealth of {{W|natural resources}}, local Araucarian governments would contract out the extraction of its mineral riches to several foreign companies in exchange for a 40% stake in the mines and refineries that were established, as well as the employment of any local Araucarians in the regions where these resources were to be extracted. Strict contracts were written up to ensure that the Illypnian powers were excluded from making claims to any resources found within Araucaria, a task made easier by the fact that Araucaria was under the protection of [[Anyos Federales|Reconstructionist Balisca]] per the [[Afro Atlantic Charter]], and was thus shielded from potential foreign attack. Unlike other non-western states that had little knowledge of the legal practices of the European governments, Araucaria would additionally negotiate from a position of strength through its knowledge of {{W|Western law}} and Baliscan backing.
The rapid influx of new migrants combined with Araucaria's high birth rate necessitated an accelerated {{W|industrialization}} process across the country, which allowed for and required an equally rapid development of the country's critical infrastructure, such as ports, factories, mills, railroads, and canals. Ports were quickly developed where the export of the natural resources of the country could be facilitated with ease, especially as the overall output of the interior mines increased with the rise of available workers. This in turn gave way to the construction of several {{W|steel mill}}s and arsenal's that would be completed by the 1870s, and fuel the expansion of Araucaria's rail network. Both the regional administrations and the confederal level led by [[Moisès Mogrovejo]] and his immediate successors would lean toward to funding of major public work initiatives, such as the Tenement Housing Act of 1870 and the [[Trans-Atlántido Railroad]] in 1881, the latter of which would serve as the catalyst of Araucaria's increasing integration into the [[Baliscan Confederation]]. 

The Confederacy official acceded to the [[Balisca|Baliscan Confederation]] at the [[1883 Confederal Convention]].

Regardless, Araucaria's industrial capacity would continue at a high rate over the course of several years, encouraged by direct investment from both the republican government and local industrial magnates, as well as by the country's rapid population growth, which approached levels similar to that witnessed in the British colonies. Members of the [[Kaftian diaspora]] from across the globe, as well as [[Asian Baliscans|Asian]] and [[Arab Baliscans|Arab]] immigrants, would leave for Araucaria for the promise of a new life and a safe and secure future; Araucaria's population would double from about three million in 1850 to more than six million by 1890. By 1890, Araucaria had become one of the most industrialized areas in Balisca, as well as the most industrialized nations anywhere in the {{W|Southern Hemisphere}}.

The turn of the 20th century ushered in a period of immense prosperity for Araucaria. Fueled by the industrial boom, major cities like [[Axarquía]], Jatiel and [[Yerba Buena]] and transformed into bustling metropolises. Araucarian magnates, like Aleix Puigmitjà, the "Hemp Baron," and Pasqual Campas, the "Steel King," built vast fortunes on the back of resource extraction and infrastructure development. However, this age of prosperity wasn't without its shadows. The rapid industrialization led to harsh working conditions, particularly for the growing [[W:Proletariat|proletariat]]. [[Trade unions in Balisca|Labor unions]], usually affiliated with the [[Federación Anárquica del Trabajo]] and the [[Confederación Autónoma del Trabajo]], began to take root, advocating for better wages and safer working environments. Strikes and protests became commonplace, challenging the status quo.

The outbreak of [[W:World War I|World War I]] in 1914 had significant repercussions for Araucaria. While the Baliscan Confederation remained neutral, Araucaria’s economy experienced disruptions due to global trade interruptions. However, the demand for raw materials and manufactured goods from neutral nations allowed Araucaria to maintain economic stability.

===''Comuncazo'' and political dissolution===
{{Main|Red Summer|Tragic Week|Baliscan Revolution}}

However, this golden age was not without its shadows. The rapid, export-driven growth led to increasing societal stratification. The [[Cataloccitan aristocracy]] in Cerdanya wielded disproportionate economic and political power at the confederal level, causing simmering unrest amongst the other republics. The {{W|First World War}} exacerbated these issues, disrupting trade and causing widespread economic turmoil.

The contentious [[1918 Cerdanyan general election]] saw the victory of Pol Pérez, a notorious crony capitalist. His election, widely condemned as fraudulent, sparked outrage across the confederation. Left-wing activists demanded the overthrow of the aristocracy and a re-run of the election, a call that quickly spread across the nation. On 28 October 1918, over four million students, laborers, and union members took to the streets in what would become known as the [[1918 Baliscan general strike]]. Major cities, including Araucaria’s vibrant coastal hubs, were brought to a standstill.

The movement gained confederal attention rapidly, especially after the assassination of key figures was linked to Pérez’s [[Progress Party (Cerdanya)|Progress Party]]. The government’s attempts to suppress the movement only fueled its growth. Protests intensified, with Concepción becoming a central hub of resistance. An anti-government gathering at the [[Universitat Cooperativa de Concepción]] ended in tragedy when the [[Civil Guard (Balisca)|Civil Guard]] attacked, resulting in the deaths of 52 people. This incident galvanized protesters, who included liberals, libertarians, feminists, socialists, and anarchists, drawing international headlines.
[[File:Araucarian Council Republic.svg|thumb|300x300px|Flag of the [[Araucarian Socialist Council Republic]] (1918)]]
The movement escalated on 21 April 1919, with the [[storming of the Baliscan Cabildo]] by rebelling soldiers supported by civilians in reaction to increasingly violent repression by the state. The successful coup against the Confederal Congress and the seizure the Cabildo [[W:Power of the deed|instigated]] the second, more violent phase of the Baliscan Revolution, ultimately leading to a significant rise in {{W|political violence}} nationwide. Tensions further escalated with the assassination of Deputy Premier [[Moisès Vilchez]] by [[Anarchism in Balisca|anarchist]] [[Miquel Cebrián]]. With the country on the verge of a violent {{W|civil war}}, the Council of Ministers narrowly voted against {{W|vote of no confidence|confidence}} in Premier [[Máximo Burjassot]], forcing the administration to dissolve. Faced with continued unrest on the streets and an increasingly demoralized and hostile military, the Confederal Council called a [[1919 Confederal Convention|Confederal Convention]]. 

The political revolution that followed was transformative. It resulted in abolished the confederal republican system and implemented {{W|anarchism|anarchist}} and {{W|Libertarian socialism|libertarian socialist}} principles. Internal borders were abolished, secularization was enacted, and {{W|Socialist self-management}} took root. Araucaria’s economy, like much of Balisca’s, was transformed with significant portions placed under {{W|worker control}}. Agrarian areas were collectivized and operated as libertarian socialist communes, while social welfare programs were expanded through income and {{W|wealth redistribution}}.

Socioeconomic policies were heavily influenced by the emerging [[W:Russian Futurism|Futurist]] movements, eventually coalescing into an [[Baliscan Accelerationism|accelerationist]] ideology. This period also accelerated the process of {{W|indigenous decolonization}} that had begun with the failed [[Cantonal Revolution]] and the subsequent [[1883 Confederal Convention]]. In Araucaria, the revolution brought about a renaissance of culture and social innovation. Worker committees took control of factories, and agrarian areas transformed into thriving, self-sustaining communes. The spirit of the revolution permeated every aspect of life, from art and literature to science and technology. Araucaria became a hub for progressive thought, attracting intellectuals and activists from around the world.
