[[File:Baliscan cannabis grower.jpg|left|thumb|320x320px|A cannabis grower in western [[Vitacura]] posing with harvested plants. Araucaria is the largest producer of [[cannabis in Balisca]] ({{Circa|2023}})]]
Araucaria maintains a [[W:Market socialism|market socialist]] economy, with a total gross state product of $1.030 trillion in 2025, placing it fourth in the country and growing by 2.5 percent relative to 2024, the fastest rate in Balisca. The [[W:Minimum wage|minimum wage]] as of 1 January 2022 was $tba an hour, the third highest in the country behind the [[Castejón Metropolis]] and [[Hesperia]]. Notable co-ops and federations in Araucaria include the design and manufacture of aircraft ([[Baliscan Aerospace Corporation]]), bicycles (Alessi), computer software development ([[Aletech]]), telecom ([[Mésmóvil]]), biotechnology, {{W|industrial hemp}} and its products (tba), beverages ([[Hierba Buena Cafe|Hierba Buena]]), clothing (tba), food products (tba), cannabis ([[Hierbería]]), and tourism. Also, significant amounts of trade with Illypnia pass through the ports of Southern Araucaria, leading to a number six ranking of Baliscan ports (ranking combines twenty-foot equivalent units (TEUs) moved and infrastructure index).

In the 1970s, [[October 76|civil unrest]], [[Camboriú War|conflict with Potocsí]] and the [[Special Period]] of {{W|energy crisis|energy}} and {{W|economic crisis|crises}} affected Araucaria disproportionately hard, leading to a period of sustained {{W|economic degrowth}}. Inline with changes in wider Baliscan society and economy, it saw the revival of rural Balisca, hastened the shift to organic agriculture, curtailed meat consumption, decreased use of automobiles, and overhauled industry, health, and diet countrywide. 

Intranational trade, facilitated by the Trans-Baliscan railway network, accounts for the majority of Araucaria's imports and exports. Araucaria is a net producer of natural and agricultural resources within Balisca, and minerals are a major export. The total untapped mineral wealth of Araucaria is estimated to be upwards of $8 trillion in 2022. Heavy industry and manufacturing, once a large portion of Araucaria's economic structure, has largely declined in favor of biotech and [[w:Environmental technology|environmental technology]].

Many coastal and former mining towns include some of the [[Social Development Index|socially developed]] areas in the Baliscan Confederation. Araucaria's regions have the highest [[W:Tax burden|tax burden]] in Balisca, reflecting strong bottom-up support for social protection programs; in 2018, total tax revenue reached 45.4% of the total GDP, while administrative social spending reached 32.8% of the GDP. Adversely, Araucaria has the lowest levels of income inequality than the rest of Balisca, and in 2020 earned a GINI score of 24.9 (compared to the Baliscan average of 32.7). 

=== Energy, infrastructure, and environment ===
{{Main|Energy in Balisca|Transport in Balisca|Environment of Balisca|AVB}}

[[File:Spain-by-plane-10 - Parque Eólico Cabimonteros.jpg|left|thumb|320x320px|Aerial view of {{W|wind farm}} in the canton of [[La Rioja]] ([[Arapytúcaá]])]]
In 2022, Araucaria was the world's fourth-largest consumer of energy, consuming some 260.76 bn {{W|watts}} (about 1798.1 W per capita, making it a {{W|2000-watt society}}), and 100% of its primary energy was domestically produced. In 2022, energy sources were: {{W|nuclear energy|nuclear}} (38.5%), {{W|wind energy|wind}} (33.6%), {{W|hydro-electric energy|hydro-electric}} (21.6%), {{W|solar energy|solar}} (3.2%), and other renewable sources (3.1%). Araucaria additionally became one of the first countries to explore tidal power in 2023, with the testing of the underwater Vicálvaro turbine generator station. As of 2022, Araucaria has 282 {{W|hydroelectric generating stations}}, most of which are located in the community's mountainous central region. The Baliscan government also implements energy conservation, green technologies, emission reduction activities, and aims to meet the country's electricity demands using 100% renewable sources by 2024. Through the Baliscan government, Araucaria is committed to the [[Kuluba Protocol]] and several other treaties promoting {{W|biodiversity}}, low emission standards, {{W|water management}}, universal {{W|recycling}}, and the {{W|renewable energy commercialization}}.

In 2016, Araucaria was the world's ?th largest exporter of {{W|silver}}, and the world's ?th largest {{W|copper}} exporter. However, Araucaria's production has steadily declined year on year due to mounting concerns over [[W:Climate change|climate change]] and the effects of mining on Araucaria's environment, along with the election of communal administrations that are more radical in relation to ecological concerns.
[[File:Seikan Tunnel Entrance Honshu side.jpg|thumb|Entrance to the [[Trans-Guadalquivir Tunnel]] from the [[Luzamontaña]] side, with it's [[W:Dual gauge|dual gauge]] tracks visible]]
Transportation has rapidly improved in Araucaria since the mining era, with the government spending billions on laying railways, and the construction of airports and the [[AVB|Baliscan high speed rail system]]. From the late 1880s Araucaria has developed an extensive system of tunnels and canals through the scattered mountain valleys of the [[Matarrañya|Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] range. While rail is the easiest way to travel around Araucaria, it has a significantly higher number of people who opt to drive with a {{W|personal vehicle}} compared to the rest of Balisca. Rising immigration, increasing density, and rapid urbanization combined with increasing environmental concerns has further discouraged dependence on roads and increasing shifts to {{W|mass transit}}. All cantons of Araucaria have adopted the [[Low Impact Initiative|''Iniciativa Bajo Impacto'']] (IBI), which has radically shifted Baliscan society away from fossil fuel dependency and car-centric development. Rooted in principles of sustainability, self-sufficiency, and communal ownership, the initiative has substantially reshaped Araucarian cities and mobility around low-emission, human-centered infrastructure, while also expanding the concept of the [[W:Right to the city|right to the city]].

Rail travel has prevailed as the primary mode of transport for Araucarians, in part due to historic confederal policies in favor of railway development, and decline of the automative industry in the face of prohibitively high gas prices in Balisca. {{W|Car ownership}} peaked in the beginning of the 1970s, before entering a rapid decline due to the [[Special Period]]. It has since become impractical and inconvenient to live in many Araucaria communes with a car. Even small-sized communes are well connected to other regions and the confederal networks via buses, while larger communes may also have transit options like [[W:Rapid transit|rapid transit]], [[W:Light rail|light rail]] and [[W:Commuter rail|commuter/suburban rail lines]] to move people around in significant numbers.

==Demographics==
{{Main|Demographics of Araucaria}}

[[File:Chile - Cochoa - 01.jpg|thumb|The [[Planned communities in Balisca|planned community]] of [[Almocatén]] in southeastern [[Juyú]], built as part of the confederal response to the [[Baliscan housing shortage]]]]
In the [[2025 Baliscan census]], Araucaria was determined to have a population of 26,340,323, and is the third-most populous community in Balisca. It's rate of population growth had been the highest in the Baliscan Confederation prior to the [[Special Period]], averaging 1.7% growth per year. By 2050 the population of Araucaria is expected to reach approximately 31 million people, remaining the third most populous community in the Confederation. About 90 percent of the country's population lives in urban areas, with around 70 percent living in on the [[Levante, Balisca|Aethiopic Coast]]. The largest agglomerations according to the 2020 census are Greater [[Axarquía]] with 4.9 million people, Greater [[Jatiel]] with 3.1 million and Greater [[Valparaíso]] with 2.3 million.

Araucaria, along with [[Hesperia]], has been one the most heavily affected by the [[Baliscan housing shortage]] as it has become one of the main points of entry for, and leading destination of, most [[W:Asylum seeker|asylum seekers]] entering the Confederation. Despite increasing immigration numbers, difficulties in housing and processing migrants, and increased pressures on local resources, Araucarians have remained overwhelming welcoming towards immigrants and refugees, with a poll in 2023 showing 83% of Araucarians in favor of welcoming migrants into their communities.

The majority of Araucarians (73.8 percent) live in family households, 15.3 percent report living alone, and those living with other relatives or unrelated persons reported at 6.8 percent. Forty-nine percent of households are couples with or without children, 6.3 percent are single-parent households, 15.6 percent are multigenerational households, and 13.8 percent are [[W:Polyamory|polyamorous]] households.

===Ancestry and ethnicity===
{{See also|Ethnography of Balisca}}[[File:Guadeloupe immigrant Araucaria.jpg|thumb|left|200px|An {{W|Afro-Caribbean}} woman from the {{W|French West Indies}} island of {{W|Guadeloupe}} in [[Carabassa]] (1911)]]
The community's most populous ethnic group, [[Kafto-Baliscans]], have declined as a proportion of the population, from a high of 60% in 1920 to 34% in 2020, as other ethnicities have increased. The second-largest ethnic group in Araucaria are [[Arab Baliscans|Arabs and Mozarabs]], who account for about one-fifth of the population. Liberated slaves from across Abyala settled throughout the territory that is now Araucaria during the colonial period, a time when roughly three-fourths of settlers came as formerly enslaved persons. Those who identify on the census as having "Araucarian ethnicity" are predominantly of Kaftian descent, but have ancestors who have been in Abyala for so long they choose to identify simply as Araucarian. Balisca was a major destination of the [[Kaftian slave trade]], and the first generations of enslaved men, women, and children were brought primarily from [[Enfolo]] in the [[South Kaftian Highlands]]. The [[W:Berber people|Imfezzans]] of Central Kaftia and {{W|Yoruba people|Imo}} ethnic groups of what is now [[Northern Ayorin]] were the single largest Kaftian groups among slaves in Balisca.
[[File:Lebanon League of Progress Executive Committee in São Paulo (1914).png|thumb|[[Arab Baliscans|Lebanese immigrants]] in [[Jatiel]] with the Lebanese flag at the time, during a summit of the [[Accelerationist International]] (1919)]]
Kafto-Baliscans in Araucaria have significantly less Illypnian ancestry than those in other Baliscan territories, and DNA analysis shows many have asymmetrical male and female ancestry contributions dating back before independence and the [[Baliscan Civil War|Civil War]], with evidence of Bayani and Imfezzan fathers and Kaftian or Indigenous Abyalan mothers both during and after the time of slavery. 

Prior to the [[1883 Confederal Convention]], Araucaria was never a particularly attractive destination for Illypnian migrants, owing to its [[Maroon Wars|historical infamy]] and relatively hostile attitude towards Illypnian immigrants. Nationalist and isolationist groups such as the [[New Araucarian League]] actively worked to discouraged Illypnian migration to Araucaria (which it denounced as inviting "passive colonization"). What little Illypnian migration that occurred did not result in any significant change in the ethnic composition of the territory. Andalusian [[Catharism|Cathars]] descended from the first [[Maroon Confederacy|Maroons]] were the only major Illypnian group in Araucaria, and their numbers were significantly reduced by emigration to [[Cerdanya]]. 

Simultaneously, Araucaria accepted significant numbers of Kaftian and Fosian migrants, with most originating from the former territories of the [[Empire of Burawa]] (including [[Ayorin]] and [[Dakare]]), alongside the [[Jade countries]], [[Okaiken|Mitoyagi]] and [[Nusaraya]]. This hastened in the 1920s and 1930s with the passage of exclusionary immigration laws in other countries in the New World. 
===Language===
{{See also|Mozarabic|Maroniko language}}[[Mozbayani language|Mozbayani]] has been the [[W:De facto|''de facto'']] [[W:Official language|official language]] of Araucaria. According to the 2024 communal survey conducted by the [[Statistics Balisca]] agency, 36.24% (8,991,420) of Araucarian residents age{{spaces}}5 and older spoke only Mozarabic at home, while 43.76% spoke another language at home. 60.35% of people who speak a language other than Mozarabic at home are able to speak Mozarabic "well" or "very well", with this figure varying significantly across the different linguistic groups. The [[Maroniko language]] is a [[W:Creole language|creole language]] spoken natively by around 9.1% (rough 2 million people) of the Araucarian population. It emerged as a result of [[W:Language contact|language contact]] between the [[W:Yoruba language|Imo]], [[Mozbayani language|Mozbayani]] and the [[Kurupirangüe language|Kurupirangüe]] languages during the [[Maroon Wars]].

[[Baliscano]] is the second-most commonly spoken language in Araucaria (behind Mozbayani), spoken by 33.47% (8,304,162) of the population (in 2024). Following Araucaria's confederation, it's linguists contributed to the standardization of the [[Baliscano language]] during the ''[[Cosmocazo]]''. It has been increasing in usage since it's confederal adoption in [[1919 Confederal Convention|1919]] and is deeply intertwined with Araucaria cultural landscape and history since confederation. The first [[Constitution of Araucaria]] was written in both Mozbayani and Maroniko at the [[Araucarian Constitutional Convention|Jatiel Constitutional Convention of 1855]].

[[Confederal initiative|Confederal initiatives]] have made significant progress in promoting [[W:Language education|language access]] and [[W:Multilingual education|multilingual education]], as have cooperative educational institutions at lower communal levels. Many of Araucaria's indigenous languages are [[W:Endangered language|endangered]], although there are now efforts toward [[W:Language revitalization|language revitalization]]. Araucaria has the highest concentration nationwide of [[Sugihan language|Sugihan]], [[Vietnamese Baliscans|Vietnamese]] and [[Punjabi Baliscans|Punjabi]] speakers.

As a result of the community's increasing diversity and migration from other areas across the confederation and around the globe, linguists began noticing a noteworthy set of emerging characteristics of spoken Baliscano in Araucaria since the late 20th century. This variety, known as [[Araucarian Baliscano]], has a [[W:Vowel shift|vowel shift]] and several other phonological processes that are different from varieties of standard Baliscano used in other regions of the confederation.

===Education===
Like the rest of Balisca, the educational system is divided into preschool (for those under age 6), basic education (9 years, in three stages, compulsory), secondary education (3 years, compulsory since 1968), and higher education (subdivided in university and polytechnic education). Starting with preschool, total immersion learning of Baliscano is a mandatory; this is reinforced further in each stage of education, with students required to pass progressively harder Baliscano exams in order to move on to the next stage.
[[File:UoS campus.png|thumb|260x260px|Campus of the [[University of Solecascada]], in [[Guadahermosa]]]]
During the time of the [[Maroon Confederacy]], access to education was rapidly expanded, as it was regarded as the foundation of a successful society. {{W|Free education}} was first open to every resident of Araucaria, both citizen and expat, and the Araucarian officials enlisted several foreign instructors to accelerate this progress. Since the 19th century, the country has progressed significantly with efforts to ensuring high [[W:Literacy|literacy]] rates, modern programs and women’s share in education.

Collectively, Araucaria's communes currently devote approximately 25 percent of total [[Communes of Balisca#communal%20spending|communal spending]] to education alone. Annual education investment went from less than $12 billion in 2000 to more than $47 billion in 2016, or 10.1% of Araucaria's gross domestic product.

Access to [[W:Public education|public]] and [[W:Cooperative education|cooperative education]] is [[W:Universal access to education|universal]] for all residents (including migrants) through the university level in Araucaria. Since the adoption of the 1883 constitution, education has been compulsory through the twelfth grade. Primary school enrollment for males in 2015 stood at 99% and 99% of females. Secondary school enrollment figures are lower with 96% for males, and 98% for females. In 2015–16 approximately 0.2 percent of students in grades one through five and 0.6 percent of students in grades six through nine did not complete their education; this rate rose to 0.9 percent in grades 10–12. The overall literacy rate of Araucaria stood at 100% in 2020. Between the ages of 15-29, males reached 99% literacy and women 100%.

The educational system is divided into preschool (for those under age 6), basic education (9 years, in three stages, compulsory), secondary education (3 years, compulsory since 1968), and higher education (subdivided in university and polytechnic education). Universities are usually organized into faculties. Institutes and schools are also common designations for autonomous subdivisions of Araucarian higher education institutions. There is no uniform curriculum across the Araucaria, with the determination left to the individual cantons. The adoption of the [[Confederal Academic Baseline Initiative]] instituted baseline standards and requirements. Communes that fail to meet the standards for a consecutive two schooling periods will have their programs abrogated by their canton.

About 51% of college-age citizens (20 years old) attend one of Araucaria's [[W:Higher education|higher education]] institutions (compared with 37% in 2010 and 29% in 2000). There is a large (albeit shrinking) gender gap: 60% of Araucarian women go on to attain higher education, compared with 48% of Araucarian men. In addition to being a destination for international students, Araucaria has also risen among the top places of origin for international students. All higher education students, both domestic and international, totaled 580,937 in 2020. Students from [[W:West Africa|West Africa]] made up the largest portion of all international students, accounting for 38% of all international attendees in 2016. Araucarian universities typically maintain strong educational partnerships with [[W:Sub-Sahara Africa|Sub-Sahara African]] and other Ibero-American institutions, and exchange programs and opportunities to study abroad are common.

Higher education in state-run educational establishments is provided on a competitive basis, a system of [[W:Numerus clausus|''numerus clausus'']] is enforced through a communal database on student admissions. However, every higher education institution offers also a number of additional vacant places through other extraordinary admission processes for sportsmen, mature applicants (over 23 years old), international students, foreign students from Latin America (especially from the Southern Cone), degree owners from other institutions, students from other institutions (academic transfer), former students (readmission), and course change, which are subject to specific standards and regulations set by each institution or course department.
===Religion===
{{Main|Religion in Balisca}}

{{See also|Irreligion in Balisca}}{{Pie chart|thumb=right|caption=Religion in Araucaria (2022)|label2={{W|Islam}} (incl. {{W|Sunni}}, {{W|Shia}} and {{W|Liberalism and progressivism within Islam|Progressive}})|value2=24.0|color2=DarkGReen|label3={{W|African diaspora religions|African Diasporic}} ([[Lucumí Tarica]], etc.)|value3=15.8|color3=DarkOrchid|label1={{W|Irreligious}}|value1=41.5|color1=DodgerBlue|label4={{W|Christianity}} (incl. [[Catharism]], {{W|Catholicism}}, [[w:Eastern Orthodox Church|Eastern]] and [[w:Oriental Orthodox Churches|Oriental Orthodox]])|value4=12.1|color4=Turquoise|label5={{W|Mythologies of the Indigenous peoples of the Americas|Indigenous}} beliefs|value5=3.3|color5=Chartreuse|label7=[[w:Hinduism|Hinduism]]|value7=1.5|color7=Orange|label8=[[w:Buddhism|Buddhism]]|value8=1.1|color8=Yellow|label6=[[w:Jewish people|Jewish]]|value6=0.1|color6=Hotpink|label9=Others/undeclared|value9=0.6|color9=Black|radius=110}}[[Basic Law: Freedom of Religious Belief|Confederal law]] bans the establishment of a {{W|state religion}} at any level of government, and prevents the government from involving itself in the private religious affairs of Baliscan citizens. Additionally, It allows the right to believe what a person, group, or {{W|religion}} wishes, but it does not allow the right to practice the religion or belief openly and outwardly in a public manner, a central facet of {{W|religious freedom}}. Since the implementation of {{W|secularism}}, the Baliscan government has co-opted the French principle of {{W|laïcité}}, in which the State does not recognize any official ''religion''. Instead, it merely recognizes certain religious organizations, according to formal legal criteria that do not address religious doctrine. In return, religious organizations are to refrain from involvement in the State's policy-making or any activity that could be interpreted as political in a court of law.
[[File:Ali bey el abbassi mosque.png|left|thumb|300x300px|The [[Ali Bey el Abbassi Islamic Cultural Center]] in [[Jatiel]]]]
In Balisca, "freedom of religious belief" is used as a more broad replacement for both "freedom of religion" and "freedom from religion". The term "belief" is considered inclusive of all forms of {{W|irreligion}}, including {{W|atheism}}, {{W|antitheism}} and {{W|humanism}}. The practice of religion is now legally considered a private matter throughout society and the state.

Islam was introduced in the Baliscan archipelago following the arrival of explorers and colonists on behalf of the [[W:Spanish Empire|Spanish Empire]] the late 15th to early 16th century, after which many were forced to choose between conversion or expulsion to the colony by the {{W|Crown of Castile}}. Significantly larger numbers of enslaved African Muslims were brought to the Baliscan islands following the beginning of the {{W|Atlantic slave trade}}. Historically, an estimated 60% of slaves brought to Balisca from {{W|West Africa|West}}/{{W|Central Africa}} were Muslims. They were overwhelmingly literate in contrast to many of the slave owners. The earliest form of education in Araucaria were Islamic [[W:Madrasa|madrasa]] established by these freemen.

Following the outbreak of the [[Maroon Wars]], a significant number of slaves rebelled across the [[Western Plains (Balisca)|Western Plains]], leading to a violent campaign of self-liberation across the agricultural heartland. Thousands escaped, some with the help of the [[Catharism|Cathar]] community which has strongly denounced the expansion of slavery. This tactic opposition and resistance eventually coalesced into the [[Maroon Confederacy]] as an independent polity.

Many freed persons became involved in the movement of refugees through the Passage, requiring extensive cooperation with the Cathar community in their activity, and often proclaimed membership while in colonial territory to reduce suspicion of their activity. Additionally, the rise of the [[Maroon pirates]] and founding of [[Daramazán]] heralded extensive exchange and cooperation between the formerly enslaved and the largely Muslim pirate clans.

According to the Baliscan Center for Sociological Research, 24.0% of Araucarian citizens self-identify as {{W|Muslim}}, (14.7% define themselves as not practicing, while 9.3% as practicing), 12.1% as [[w:Christianity|Christian]] (any denomination), 22.4% as followers of other faiths (including {{W|African diaspora religions}}, {{W|Mythologies of the Indigenous peoples of the Americas|Indigenous beliefs}}, {{W|Hinduism}}, {{W|Buddhism}} etc.), and 31.5% identify as {{W|atheists}} (20.9%) and [[agnostics]] (10.6%) as of January 2022. According to the same poll, the proportion of the unaffiliated is higher among the youth, about 89% among the 20-years old.

An overwhelming majority of Araucarians do not participate regularly in weekly religious worship. A July 2021 study shows that of the Araucarians who identify themselves as religious, 66% never attend services, 20.8% barely ever attend services, 19% attend a few times a year, 6.8% two or three times per month, 13.4% every holidays, and 0.6% multiple times per week.

== Culture ==
{{See also|Culture of Balisca}}
[[File:Traditional Araucarian dress.png|thumb|300x300px|Araucarians in ceremonial [[W:Folk costume|folk attire]], during a ''[[Day of the Liberated|Día Liberados]]'' festival in western [[Juyú]]]]
Araucaria's culture draws influences from its broad range of constituent ethnicities, and longstanding policies that have promoted a "{{W|just society}}" are constitutionally protected. Araucaria, through confederal-level initiatives, has placed emphasis on liberation and [[W:Self-determination|self-determination]] for all its people. This longstanding [[W:Cosmopolitanism|cosmopolitan]] {{W|multiculturalism}} is often cited as one of Balisca's significant accomplishments, and a key distinguishing contribution of Baliscan identity. In Araucaria, cultural identity is strong, and there is a [[Afro-Baliscans|Afro-Baliscan culture]] that is often incorrectly conflated with the wider Araucaria culture. As a whole, Araucaria is in theory a {{W|cultural mosaic}} of regional ethnic subcultures.
[[File:Baliscan agitprop.png|left|thumb|368x368px|"Solidarity within the [[W:Global South|Global South]]!", a [[Mozarabic]]-language [[W:Agitprop|agitprop]] produced in Araucaria]]
Post-revolutionary approaches to governance have emphasized [[W:Polyculturalism|polyculturalism]], which is based on {{W|free migration}}, {{W|social integration}} and {{W|social equity|equity}}, and [[W:Anti-authoritarianism|opposition to authoritarianism]]. Initiatives have included [[W:Mutual aid|mutual aid]], {{W|universal healthcare|socially funded health care}}, universal {{W|right to housing|access to housing}} and {{W|right to food}}, [[W:Mutualism (economic theory)|mutualism]], the abolition of {{W|capital punishment}}, social efforts to eliminate poverty, and a socially permissive attitude towards self-identification and determination across a wide array of subjects. This includes [[Abortion in Balisca|abortion]], [[LGBT rights in Balisca|LGBTQ rights]], [[Euthanasia in Balisca|legalized euthanasia]], and longstanding [[Cannabis in Balisca|cannabis cultivation]] and consumption. Araucarians also contributed greatly to Balisca's [[Baliscan internationalism|internationalist]] orientation, [[Constitution of Balisca|confederal organization]] and the [[1883 Confederal Convention|Confederal Declaration on Rights and Freedoms]].

Since the beginning of [[Colonial Balisca|European colonization]] of Balisca, the culture of Araucaria has been influenced by {{W|African diaspora|African}}, {{W|Mozarabic}}, and [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|Indigenous]] cultures and traditions. Through their language, art and music, these diverse cultures gradually formed a [[W:Creole|creole]] culture and identity. Beginning in the 19th and 20th centuries, the migration of Asian, Caribbean and have added to the Araucarian identity and its culture. 

[[Rural society in Balisca|Traditional Araucaria]] is strongly associated with the image of the [[guaso|''guaso'']], or "cowboy", which historically pioneered the [[Maroon Wars|wars of resistance]] against colonial encroachment on frontiers of the territory. Themes and symbols of outlaws, trappers, and traders played an important part in the early development of the emerging Araucarian identity. Modern Araucarian culture as it is understood today can be traced to its origin in social crisis, resistance and eventual difficult nation building. Contributing factors also include Araucaria's unique geography.

=== Cuisine ===
{{See also|Baliscan cuisine}}[[File:Araucarian akara.jpg|left|thumb|[[W:Akara|''Akara'']], made from raw and milled [[W:Cowpea|cowpeas]] that are seasoned with salt, pepper and chopped onions molded into the shape of a large scone and deep-fried in [[W:Palm oil|palm oil]]. It is served split in half and stuffed with {{W|vatapá}} and {{W|caruru|kalalu}} – spicy pastes made from shrimp, ground cashews, palm oil and other ingredients.]]
Following the discovery of America by Spanish explorers, the [[W:Columbian Exchange|Columbian Exchange]] began, introducing previously unknown foods to the Old World, such as [[W:Potatoes|potatoes]], [[W:Tomatoes|tomatoes]], and [[W:Maize|maize]]. In modern Araucaria, the cuisine often features staple ingredients like corn, potatoes, and [[W:Chili peppers|chilies]], deeply rooted in Indigenous traditions. The diverse climate of Araucaria supports the cultivation of over 3,000 varieties of potatoes, as noted by the [[Instituto Araucario de la Papa|''Instituto Araucario de la Papa'']].

Modern Araucarian cuisine is a fusion of Amerindian and Mediterranean culinary traditions, with strong influences from other cultures, including Chinese, African, Arab, Italian, and Japanese. Common dishes in Araucaria include [[W:Choripán|choripán]], [[W:Ceviche|ceviche]], and [[W:Empanadas|empanadas]], which highlight the region's rich culinary heritage. 

The cuisine of Araucaria is a testament to the blend of Indigenous practices and the influences brought by colonizers and immigrants. Lacking the familiar ingredients from their homelands, immigrants adapted their traditional recipes by incorporating locally available foods. The four traditional staples of Araucarian cuisine are corn, potatoes and other tubers, [[W:Amaranthaceae|Amaranthaceaes]] ([[W:Quinoa|quinoa]], kañiwa, and kiwicha), and legumes (beans and lupins). Old World colonizers introduced additional staples like rice, wheat, and meats (beef, pork, and chicken). 

In recent years, there has been a revival of interest in Native Araucarian foods and culinary techniques, with traditional ingredients such as [[W:Quinoa|quinoa]], [[W:Amaranthus caudatus|amaranto]], chili peppers, and various roots and tubers gaining popularity. In cities like Jatiel and Daramazán, traditional dishes are often served with a modern twist, appealing to both locals and tourists. 

=== Music ===
{{main|Araucarian music}}

[[File:La Jarana - Ignacio Merino s. XIX.jpg|thumb|331x331px|''La Jarana'' (lit. The Revelry), c. 1857]]
Araucarian music has [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|Indigenous]], [[W:Music of Spain|Spanish]] (particularly Andalusian) and [[W:African Music|African]] roots. In pre-Columbian times, musical expressions varied widely in each region; the ''[[quena]]'' and the ''[[tinya]]'' were two common instruments. Spaniards introduced new instruments, such as the guitar and the harp, which led to the development of crossbred instruments like the ''[[W:Charango|charango]]''. African contributions to Araucarian music include its rhythms, [[W:Candombe|c''andombe'']], a style of dance and the ''[[W:Cajon|cajón]]'', a percussion instrument.

Araucarian music is dominated by the national [[W:Musical instrument|instrument]], the [[cavaquiño]]. The cavaquiño is a member of the [[W:Lute|lute]] family of instruments and was invented during [[Colonial Balisca|colonial times]] by musicians imitating the Spanish [[W:Vihuela|vihuela]]. In the [[Arapytúcaá]] and [[Hibiscara]] regions, the cavaquiño is also used in courtship rituals, symbolically invoking mermaids with the instrument to lure the woman to the male performers. 

While the Spanish guitar is widely played, so too is the Andalusian-in-origin [[W:Bandurria|bandurria]]. Unlike the guitar, it has been transformed by Peruvian players over the years, changing from a 12-string, 6-course instrument to one having 12 to 16 strings in a mere four courses. Violins and [[W:Harps|harps]], also of European origin, are also played. A very famous instrument throughout Araucaria is the [[W:Pan flute|Pan flute]], dating back to pre-colonial times times. It is made of hollow bamboo tubes and is widely played in the Araucarian [[Matarrañya]].

===Outdoor life and athletics===
[[File:Tamajón coast.jpg|thumb|The coast of the [[Tamajón]] regions is considered some of the best for {{W|surfing}} year round|left]]Given its varied hilly and mountainous terrain and its coasts, lakes, rivers, and forests, Araucaria has long been enjoyed for pursuits like hiking and camping, {{W|rock climbing}} and mountaineering, {{W|hunting}} and {{W|fishing}}. Water sports, both motorized and non-motorized, are enjoyed in many places. {{W|Sea kayak}}ing opportunities abound on the Southern coast with its {{W|fjord}}s and [[W:ria|rias]]. {{W|Whitewater rafting}} and {{W|kayak}}ing are popular on many inland rivers. {{W|Surfing}}, {{W|Sailing}} and [[w:Windsurfing|sailboarding]] are widely enjoyed. [[File:Baliscan guaso.png|thumb|285x285px|An Afro-Mozarab [[guaso|''guaso'']] in traditional attire, taken in western [[Tukupi]] (1938)]]In urban areas, opportunities for [[w:jogging|joggers]] and bicyclists have long existed. Cross-country bike touring has been popular since the [[w:Bicycle gearing|ten-speed]] bike became available many years ago. Since the advent of the more robust {{W|mountain bike}}, trails in more rugged and extreme areas have been developed for them. A 2016 poll on global biking website Pinkbike rated Araucaria as one of the top destinations mountain bikers would like to ride. In 1998 Araucaria's region embarked on the development of the world's first cross-country {{W|bicycle highway}}. {{W|Longboarding}} is also a popular activity because of its hilly geography.

[[w:Equestrianism|Horseback riding]] is enjoyed by a significant proportion of the population, typically in the rural interior regions. Opportunities for {{W|trail riding}}, often into especially scenic areas, have long existed across the territory, dating back to pre-colonial times and the [[Maroon Wars]].

Araucaria also has strong representation in many other sports, including {{W|tennis}}, [[w:Association football|soccer]], [[w:ice hockey|hockey]], {{W|American football}}, {{W|rugby union}}, {{W|futsal}}, {{W|baseball}}, {{W|Basque pelota}}, {{W|basketball}}, {{W|curling}}, {{W|disc golf}} and {{W|figure skating}}. Araucaria has produced many outstanding athletes, especially in aquatic and summer sports.

Consistent with both increased environmental interest and increased participation in diverse conservation efforts by Baliscan residents, has been the decline of [[w:Resort hotel|lodges]], {{W|chalet}}s, {{W|bed and breakfast}}s, motels, hotels, fishing camps versus an increase in park-camping facilities in recent decades. In certain areas, there are businesses, non-profit societies, or municipal governments dedicated to promoting {{W|ecotourism}} in their region. With the adoption of the 2022 [[Baliscan Constitution]], the Araucarian government has recognized farmers whom visitors to combine tourism with farm work, for example, through the {{W|WWOOF}} Balisca program, and the national [[Homestay Collective]].
==Geography==
{{Main|Geography of Araucaria}}

[[File:Itaimbezinho - Parque Nacional Aparados da Serra 09.JPG|thumb|270x270px|{{W|Araucaria angustifolia}} (''candelabra tree''), the community's namesake, in [[Vitacura]]'s [[Candelabra National Park|''C''andelabra National Park]].]]
Araucaria is located at the southern extremity of the island of [[Atlantida]], making it one of the four Atlántidan communities. About {{convert|10|km}} of the [[Strait of Ivorra]] separates Araucaria and the rest of [[Atlantida]] from [[Cerdanya]] on the island of [[Tinguiririca]]. Araucaria's most populous city, [[Axarquía]], is situated on the southern shore the Strait of Ivorra, opposite of the city of [[Concepción]] (Cerdanya). Araucaria is peninsula, being bounded by the {{W|South Atlantic Ocean}} to the east; the {{W|Argentine Sea}} to the west; and the Strait of Ivorra to the south.

At {{convert|168,372.7|km2|0}}, Araucaria is the fourth largest community in the Baliscan Confederation, and is slightly less than twice the size of the smallest community, [[Estuaria]]. Additionally, it is the 48th [[w:List of the largest country subdivisions by area|largest subnational division in the world]], and would be the 16th largest country in the world. The [[Matarrañya|Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] and the dramatic coastlines provide some of Balisca's most renowned and spectacular scenery, which forms the backdrop and context for a growing outdoor adventure and [[W:Ecotourism|ecotourism]] industry. 65 percent of the Araucaria is mountainous (more than 1,000 metres (3,300 ft) above sea level); 60 percent is forested; and only about 11 percent is arable.

The [[Cabo Traïdor]] peninsula juts southwest from the mainland. Many seismically active {{W|fault line}}s are found in the central and eastern Araucaria, especially in the coastal regions. The region of [[Vitacura]], for example, is crossed by the [[Yawar Sypa]] fault, which was responsible for the [[1982 Tava Morotí earthquake]] and caused significant damage across the [[Alpujarra]].<gallery widths="200" perrow="3" mode="packed" heights="80" caption="The varied landscapes of Araucaria">
